      "text": "We can count the number of ways to choose 3 marbles out of 7 by listing all possible combinations.\n\nSolution 1:\nWe can use the combination formula to count the number of ways to choose 3 marbles out of 7: $\\binom{7}{3} = \\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} = \\frac{7!}{3!4!} = \\frac{7\\times 6\\times 5}{3\\times 2} = 35$.\n\nTherefore, Johnny can choose 3 marbles out of 7 in $\\boxed{35}$ ways.\n\nSolution 2:\nWe can also count the number of ways to choose 3 marbles out of 7 by listing all possible combinations.\n\nSolution 2.1:\nFor the first marble, there are 7 choices (any of the 7 marbles).\nFor the second marble, there are 6 choices (any of the remaining marbles, except the first marble).\nFor the third marble, there are 5 choices (any of the remaining marbles, except the first two marbles).\n\nTherefore, there are $7\\times 6\\times 5 = 210$ ways to choose 3 marbles out of 7, but since the order of choosing the marbles does not matter, we must divide by 3!: $\\boxed{35}$.",
